00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:06.230 align:start position:0%
these images show some of more than 800  police intelligence officers carrying  out an unprecedented raid in south  central chile  in an area dominated by indigenous  mapuches  on this farm police searching for  marijuana were attacked by unidentified  gunmen  one of the officers was killed although  his death  dominated national news it wasn't the  only violence that took place that day  these photos show police operatives  using what human rights experts call  illegitimate use of force and abuse  against minors  here the target was the seven-year-old  daughter of mapuche activist camilo  catrianka  he was killed two years ago by special  forces police  her mother and grandmother were also  thrown face down to the ground and  detained  this is not an exceptional or isolated  case it's part of  institutional violence practiced against  the mapuche people  in which children are also victims  police intelligence director hector  espinosa  denies that the child was detained and  insists that chilean police don't  mistreat miners  but this recording of a mapuche teenager  reportedly being grabbed by police  suggests otherwise you can hear the girl  being told she'll be killed if she  doesn't shut up  a prosecutor is now investigating  charges of alleged police abuse against  minors on the day of the massive raid  but with the government now under strong  pressure to declare  a state of siege in the rest of region a  lot of people  are afraid that things will get worse in  fact the army has already been given the  go-ahead to carry out patrols in the  area  alongside special forces police  situation the situation today is of  uncertainty  and fear for the safety of the children  the violation of mapuche children's  human rights is an intergenerational  problem  the children who yesterday experienced  violence from the state  are the parents of the children  experiencing it today  so the job of reparation and  reconciliation is very complex  the sharp increase in attacks by  unidentified armed groups  who claim to fight for mapuche land  rights has taken the conflict in  southern chile to a new level  and with no solution in sight a new  generation of mapuche children is  growing up with violence  fear and anger lucia newman al jazeera  santiago
 

